import{_ as n,q as o,o as s,c as r,b as t,k as i,j as h,m as d}from"./chunks/framework.D3-xQjZb.js";const y=JSON.parse('{"title":"What Could You Learn If You Could Listen to Every Student Work?","description":"Turn an assignment into an AI Studio, collect student explanations as they work, and use AI-assisted grading to plan individual feedback and the next class.","frontmatter":{"title":"What Could You Learn If You Could Listen to Every Student Work?","description":"Turn an assignment into an AI Studio, collect student explanations as they work, and use AI-assisted grading to plan individual feedback and the next class.","teaser":"Give an AI coach an assignment you already teach. What students say while working can help you grade one project and decide what to teach the whole class tomorrow.","articleGenre":"Classroom feature","imageCaption":"A teacher can design the question and decide which responses are worth bringing back to class.","socialImage":"/images/editorial/social-field-desk.jpg","socialImageAlt":"An open notebook with hand-drawn diagrams and a red pencil on a blue desk.","socialImageWidth":1200,"socialImageHeight":630},"headers":[],"relativePath":"teaching/when-ai-helps-write-the-code.md","filePath":"teaching/when-ai-helps-write-the-code.md"}'),c={name:"teaching/when-ai-helps-write-the-code.md"};function l(u,e,p,g,m,w){const a=o("ArticleHeader");return s(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"what-could-you-learn-if-you-could-listen-to-every-student-work",tabindex:"-1"},[i("What Could You Learn If You Could Listen to Every Student Work? "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#what-could-you-learn-if-you-could-listen-to-every-student-work","aria-label":'Permalink to "What Could You Learn If You Could Listen to Every Student Work?"'},"​")],-1)),h(a),e[1]||(e[1]=d('<p>One of the best reasons to pull up a chair beside a student is to hear the story behind a choice. Imagine a student pointing to a size chart on a website they built. “I put the measurements at the top. Then I tried to buy the shirt and realized the size button took me right past them.” Now I have something to teach with. I know what the student expected, what surprised them, and why they changed the page. While I’m helping one student, I miss exchanges like that at the next table.</p><p>That’s why I make assignments I call <em>Studios</em>. I can take something I already teach, give an AI coach a few good questions to ask while the student works, and decide which responses belong in the hand-in. A student gets to practice explaining a real decision to someone who can ask a follow-up, the way an interviewer might. When I have more than a hundred students to teach, those same responses help me decide what to say to one student and what to take up with the whole class.</p><h2 id="start-with-an-assignment-you-already-teach" tabindex="-1">Start with an assignment you already teach <a class="header-anchor" href="#start-with-an-assignment-you-already-teach" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Start with an assignment you already teach&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open an ordinary AI chat, paste in the teacher guide and an assignment, and type <strong>“Help me turn this into a Studio.”</strong> The guide asks which decision you want students to learn to make. It drafts a short lesson for them and instructions for a coach that can ask a question, offer a hint, and wait for the reply. You can use your existing rubric and submission process. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/kaw393939.github.io/blob/main/examples/ai-studio-starter/TEACHER-STUDIO.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the teacher guide</a></p><p>Before handing it out, I’d play a student for one turn. “I’m stuck. Pick for me.” If the coach makes the decision I wanted the student to practice, I can change that line and try again.</p><h2 id="bring-a-job-posting-to-the-studio" tabindex="-1">Bring a job posting to the Studio <a class="header-anchor" href="#bring-a-job-posting-to-the-studio" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Bring a job posting to the Studio&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Ask students to bring an internship posting they’d actually apply for. With the posting and the assignment in the same chat, I can ask the teacher guide, <em>“Which responsibility in this job could a student practice through this assignment? Give me one question that would help me hear them doing it.”</em></p><p>One posting in my career research was for an AI Operations intern at NVIDIA. The intern would build a tool with AI assistance, gather feedback from people using it, and find out whether it improved their work. That last responsibility changes what I’d ask the student with the size chart to do. <a href="https://nvidia.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/NVIDIAExternalCareerSite/job/US-CA-Santa-Clara/AI-Operations-Intern--AI-Clouds---Summer-2027_JR2029119-1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the internship description</a></p><p>Hand the store to a classmate. Say, “Pick a shirt that fits you.” Let the shopper decide where to click. Then the coach can ask the designer, <strong>“What did the shopper do that changed your mind?”</strong> If the designer moves the measurements, hand the page back and try the same task again. The student now has a user observation, a change in the project, and a result they can talk about in an interview.</p><h2 id="decide-what-you-want-to-hear" tabindex="-1">Decide what you want to hear <a class="header-anchor" href="#decide-what-you-want-to-hear" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decide what you want to hear&quot;">​</a></h2><p>I design these assignments around <strong>Research → Plan → Build → Test → Explain</strong>. Each stage gives the coach a reason to ask a different question. In the Knowledge Lab, a student building an app that answers questions from documents can begin by reading a source and finding a question it can answer. During planning, the coach might ask, “Which passage do you expect your app to find?” After the student builds and tests the app, the coach can return to that prediction. “What did it actually return? Show me the words that support your answer.” The student can set the passages side by side and explain what they would keep or change. <a href="/knowledge-lab/students/research" target="_self">Try the Knowledge Lab research step</a>.</p><p>You choose how much of that conversation to collect. The general starter lets a learner submit a short exchange, annotate the work, or explain a choice aloud. My Brand Studio gives a more structured example. Its coach drafts a learning record with the student’s words or a labeled paraphrase, the help they received, and a link to the work or check they’re discussing. The student can correct the record before submitting it with their work. A brief answer like “yes” leaves room for a follow-up. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/is117-brand-studio/blob/main/LEARNING.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">See the Brand Studio learning record</a></p><h2 id="turn-the-peer-demo-into-an-interview-rehearsal" tabindex="-1">Turn the peer demo into an interview rehearsal <a class="header-anchor" href="#turn-the-peer-demo-into-an-interview-rehearsal" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Turn the peer demo into an interview rehearsal&quot;">​</a></h2><p>At Rootly, product-engineering candidates submit a working app, their AI session transcripts, and a short walkthrough. Its CTO describes a candidate who spotted a problem with how generated code handled a retry from Slack. Canva interviewers also describe watching candidates question AI suggestions as they work. Datadog tells candidates which rounds permit AI and expects them to explain their work when the tool is closed. A Studio gives students room to practice explaining a choice while working with AI and answering a fresh question on their own. <a href="https://rootly.com/blog/re-thinking-candidates-take-homes-in-the-ai-era-transcripts-over-code" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Rootly’s take-home</a> · <a href="https://www.canva.dev/blog/engineering/ai-interview-success/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Canva’s interviewer guide</a> · <a href="https://careers.datadoghq.com/candidate-experience/interviewing-at-datadog-ai-guidelines/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Datadog’s candidate guidance</a></p><p>I’d use the peer demo students are already doing. With the project and AI tool open, the designer walks a partner through the size-chart change. The partner picks a detail and asks, <em>“What did the AI suggest here? What did you decide to keep or change? Show me how you checked it.”</em> The designer can open the page, replay the interaction, or point to a specific edit.</p><p>Then close the chat. The partner changes one condition the designer hasn’t rehearsed. <em>“The shopper was using a laptop. Suppose they’re holding a phone in one hand. What would you test first, and why?”</em> Switch roles and let the next interviewer choose a different follow-up. Everyone gets a turn while I circulate. If a student can retell the first decision but gets stuck on the new one, we’ve found something specific to practice before a real interview. <a href="/ai-careers#how-employers-interview-with-ai">Compare employer interview formats</a></p><h2 id="grade-one-project-then-read-across-the-class" tabindex="-1">Grade one project, then read across the class <a class="header-anchor" href="#grade-one-project-then-read-across-the-class" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Grade one project, then read across the class&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="when-you-teach-more-than-100-students" tabindex="-1">When you teach more than 100 students <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-you-teach-more-than-100-students" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When you teach more than 100 students&quot;">​</a></h3><p>With more than a hundred students across sections, I can’t sit beside everyone for that conversation. I can give everyone the rehearsal and still hear enough of each person’s reasoning to give useful feedback. The workload decision happens when I design the hand-in. Students keep one moment worth discussing beside the project or rubric entry they already submit. For the storefront, that might be the shopper’s action, the designer’s reason for moving the measurements, and a link to the revision or retest. They can annotate the work or show it during their usual demo.</p><p>For grading, I’d start with one section’s submissions and my rubric. I’d give the AI this instruction.</p><blockquote><p>Give me one row per submission. For each student, point to their explanation, the part of the project it refers to, and any evidence of what happened when they tried it. Identify one question if the record leaves something open. Leave missing evidence marked as missing.</p></blockquote><p>I can read a row beside the relevant part of the project, check the suggested feedback, and decide the grade. A student who writes “I moved the measurements” has given me a precise place to follow up. <em>“What happened when you handed the shopper the revised page?”</em> A student who reports that the shopper still missed the chart on the second try has done something worth recognizing. They found a limit to their fix and can explain what they’d try next. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/is117-brand-studio/blob/main/docs/assessment.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">See the instructor’s assessment guide</a></p><p>For the section overview I’d ask, <em>“Which students documented a change and left the retest open? Which responses show what a shopper actually did the second time? Link me to an example behind each pattern.”</em> If several students in one section stopped at the edit, I can use one anonymous example and give the whole room a chance to retest. If another section is already doing that well, I can spend its demo time asking students to defend a tradeoff on a fresh case. A question particular to one student belongs in that student’s feedback.</p><p>If you try this, start with a question you already ask when you pull up a chair beside a student. Build it into an assignment. When you review the replies, look for one that changes the feedback you give that student and one that changes what you teach next. <a href="/downloads/AI-Studio-Starter.zip">Download the teacher Studio, including worked answers</a> · <a href="/teaching/studio-field-notes">Read the teaching field notes</a></p>',24))])}const b=n(c,[["render",l]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
